WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.810
Hey David, how can I stop drowning in email?

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.549
My email inbox is out of control.

00:00:06.549 --> 00:00:10.920
It feels like I spent all day on email and
don't actually get my job done.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.920
WTF.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.270
Okay, so listen there, there are endless strategies
to deal with this.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.410
A common, I'm not going to go through all
the ones that we hear, but a common one that

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:27.810
you hear is check email only at preset times,
check email at 9:00 AM and then 4:00 PM and

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.210
then that's it.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:34.740
And then very often when people get that advice,
they will say, but in my job I'm not able

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.740
to do that.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.650
Now, a lot of times the people who say in
my job, I'm not able to do that.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:46.140
They actually would be able to do it if they
really thought through a way to generate expectations

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.710
that you're not going to be on email all day,
but this is a real problem.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:55.300
There are lots of jobs where 50 60 70% of
the day is going to just keeping up with email

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.230
rather than actually doing the value generation
of the job.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.350
So let me concede to you.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.590
You are in one of those jobs where you can't
just check email a few a couple of times a

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:06.869
day or something like that.

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:09.720
I have successfully cut down my email time.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.070
I would estimate by 75 or 80% with a very
simple three step strategy.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.070
Okay.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:21.350
The strategy is auto filtering, manual triaging,
and then batch processing.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.350
Okay.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.479
It sounds complicated.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:24.522
It's not complicated at all.

00:01:24.522 --> 00:01:25.780
All right.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.630
First you start with auto filtering.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.320
Any for, I'll talk about for me, you apply
this to yourself.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.210
I'm on a bunch of different newsletters that
contain articles I might want to read or are

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.140
stories that might be good for the show.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.340
I've got wrap-up services of what happened
on, you know, what happened on Fox news, all

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.340
these different things.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.340
So I can pick things that I want to use for
the show.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.799
Uh, anything related to, we get all sorts
of emails, I mean thousands and thousands

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:52.639
of emails every month about people's membership
payments that came in, people's membership

00:01:52.639 --> 00:01:56.319
payments that failed, new subscriptions, cancellations,
all that stuff.

00:01:56.319 --> 00:01:58.979
Everything I just listed gets auto filtered
into folders.

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:04.290
All of my newsletter services are automatically
filtered into an out of my inbox and into

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.290
a folder.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.690
Everything that's membership related gets
filtered into a separate folder.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.299
I never see that stuff in my inbox.

00:02:12.299 --> 00:02:14.200
That is 90% of my email right there.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.200
Great.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.620
I'm nine tenths of the way there.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.570
Then you've got to manually triage.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.570
Okay.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.319
I go through email more often than some people
and maybe I don't need to, but we do have

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:26.220
things that come up with the production of
the show.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.730
Tech issues might have to be in touch with
affiliates or sales issues where I need to

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.790
get involved in like a, you know, figuring
out whether an advertiser is a good fit for

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:36.590
it for us or not or something like that.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.260
So I need to see if those urgent things come
in.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.569
But those take under 30 seconds to respond
to.

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:42.569
Okay.

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:45.879
And what my goal is is I never want to look
at an email more than two times.

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:50.510
I look at it the first time, can I answer
this email in under 30 seconds if it even

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.510
needs a response?

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.620
If the answer is yes, I do it.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:58.099
Some people say you do your ten second responses,
everything else gets triaged.

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:03.469
If the email takes more than 30 seconds for
me to answer, I asked myself, does this have

00:03:03.469 --> 00:03:06.610
to be done today or does this have to be done
this week?

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:11.640
If it's longer than this week, I file it and
put a note in my calendar to revisit it when

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.680
it needs to be revisited.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.480
If it's an email that needs to be dealt with
today, I move it to a folder called today.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.469
If it's something that needs to be done this
week, I move it to a folder called this week

00:03:21.469 --> 00:03:27.030
and then at the end of the day I'll go to
my today folder and one after the other deal

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:33.080
with every email that had to be answered today
I'm condensing the email answering periods

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.260
of the day drastically.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.380
Anything that's under 30 seconds that really
is of a more urgent nature, even though we

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.030
could debate whether it's really that urgent
or not, uh, it gets answered right away.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.469
And then I look at my auto filtered stuff,
membership emails, those newsletters, articles,

00:03:47.469 --> 00:03:48.569
stuff like that.

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:54.790
Once a day or every other day or whatever,
this has cut down to 75% on the time I spend

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:55.790
on email.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:56.970
That's my suggestion.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.790
There are services that can also help with
this stuff.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:03.249
There are some services that will gather similar
types of emails and keep them out of your

00:04:03.249 --> 00:04:05.310
inbox until you want them delivered.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.560
You might say, deliver these at 4:30 PM and
then you get them all at once.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.359
For example, I've not found the need to use
those services, but I know people who have

00:04:13.359 --> 00:04:14.719
found them useful.

00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:15.719
That is my approach.

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:20.199
Critique it, use whatever of it might be useful
for you, reduce the amount of time you're

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:23.610
spending on email because listen, I want to
be spending time researching stuff for the

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:32.710
show, generating value, creating content,
and when I cut down my email, 75% uh, I'm

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.900
drastically increasing the amount of time
I can spend on just making the best possible

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.080
show.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.460
We've got a great bonus show coming up for
you today.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.770
Make sure to sign up at David pakman.com/membership
or at patrion.com/david Pakman show.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.259
I'll speak to you then the David Pakman show@davidpakman.com
[inaudible].

